Z||000005||florid87gt||04-01-2001||09:48 PM||||I need a 142 (12" dia.) tooth neutrally balanced flywheel the crank bolt circle doesn't even matter for I won't be using it I asked for the part number on the 88 2.8 and someone was nice enough to give me the part number but when I checked with gmpartsdirect.com it came up as part not available. So I thought if the 2.5 used a neutrally balanced flywheel I could use that instead. This is the reasoning behind my question. sorry I wasn't clear the first time. Thanks.<P>I was also just told in my other thread that the 3.4 TDC is neutrally ballanced is ths correct and would anyone know the part number for that. Thanks again<p>[This message has been edited by florid87gt (edited 04-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||bubbajoexx||04-01-2001||10:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by florid87gt:<BR><B>I need a 142 (12" dia.) tooth neutrally balanced flywheel the crank bolt circle doesn't even matter for I won't be using it I asked for the part number on the 88 2.8 and someone was nice enough to give me the part number but when I checked with gmpartsdirect.com it came up as part not available. So I thought if the 2.5 used a neutrally balanced flywheel I could use that instead. This is the reasoning behind my question. sorry I wasn't clear the first time. Thanks.<P>I was also just told in my other thread that the 3.4 TDC is neutrally ballanced is ths correct and would anyone know the part number for that. Thanks again<P>[This message has been edited by florid87gt (edited 04-01-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>if you are looking for a neutral balance flywheel use the 88 to 94 Z24 flywheel they are all neutral balanced 2.8 or 3.1||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voytek||04-02-2001||02:28 PM||||Same neutrally balanced flywheel all the way to '97, 3.1.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rprevo||04-02-2001||09:42 PM|||| For whatever it is worth I had two<BR>Ironduke flywheels externally balanced for<BR>chevy 1 piece rear main seal smallblocks.<BR>If you don't have the crank out of the engine<BR>that you want to bolt it to borrow one from the local crankshaft place and tell them<BR>you need to talk to there balance man,<BR>I was told by redford speed in Michigan that<BR>if your staying under 6000 rpm's you can<BR>get away with duplicating your assembly<BR>with a good reground STRAIGHT crank.<BR>The Fiero I did last year has 5000 miles on it and the engine is around 360 hp the other<BR>was a brand new GM 350 crate fully assembled<BR>and I have had no problems with any vibrations at all. The way to tell which way to balance your rotateing assembly is to ask someone that balances things for a living.<BR>That is what I did and was very satisfied.<P> E-Mail me if you need some Phone numbers I called plenty when I did the first project.<P>    Ray<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||batboy||04-02-2001||10:00 PM||||Just to clarify and to slightly correct what Wolfhound said, the 2.5 "balancer" Duke that has the internal oil filter was installed in a few 1987 Fieros (just in the last few months of that model year production) and all 1988 Fieros. I have a 87 SE with a 2.5 that is a "coil pack" DIS Duke, but is not a "balancer" engine. That's why Ogre said there was three different 2.5 motors used in Fieros. Prior to 87, all the 2.5 engines had distributors.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">    <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, battery<BR>relocated up front, stereo mods, etc...||||reg||4
Z||000010||Wolfhound||04-03-2001||06:45 AM||||Batboy, Interesting, I didn't realize the interim engine existed. The one 87 I've been inside was internally balanced.||||reg||1
